      NE: 'Secretary of State figures, cited from The New York Times tracker. In the Senate race, the Republican incumbent\'s main opponent is an independent, so party registration says even less than elsewhere about how people will vote.',
      GA: 'Georgia does not register voters by party. The split comes from past participation in party primaries, as estimated by The New York Times; "unaffiliated and other" here means voters who have never voted in a primary. Black voters make up 32% of early voters, against 30% at the same date in 2022.',
    },
    ncTitle: 'North Carolina: who the early voters are',
    ncLede: 'The state publishes the age and party of every voter, and the turnout history of every registered voter.',
    ageTitle: 'Age', histTitle: 'Turnout in the two previous November elections', raceTitle: 'Race reported at registration',
    age: { '18_29': '18 to 29', '30_44': '30 to 44', '45_64': '45 to 64', '65_plus': '65 and over' },
    hist: { regular: 'Both', intermittent: 'One', new_or_inactive: 'Neither' },
    histNote: 'For 2026, the two previous elections are 2022 and 2024; for 2022, 2018 and 2020; for 2024, 2020 and 2022. "Neither" groups newly registered voters and people who had stayed home.',
    newParty: (d: number, r: number, u: number) => `Among 2026 voters who voted in neither 2022 nor 2024, ${pc(d, 0)} are registered Democrats, ${pc(r, 0)} Republicans and ${pc(u, 0)} unaffiliated or other.`,
    race: { white: 'White', black: 'Black', hispanic: 'Hispanic', other: 'Other', unknown: 'Not reported' },
    rateTitle: 'Share of regular voters who have already voted',
